analysis:
This PR moves inline Postgres schema DDL into a migration baseline. The head of coordinator/store/cacheroutingstate_postgres.go has no schema constants left, so the DDL for cache_routing_holders and cache_routing_demand now lives elsewhere. coordinator/store/global_payouts_postgres.go drops globalPayoutSchema (lines 13-28 of the base), which created global_payout_recipients and global_payout_withdrawals, added the expires_at column, backfilled it for quoted rows, and created the unique external_id index and the reconcile and quote-expiry indexes. coordinator/store/machine_inventory_schema.go is deleted entirely. The test file now calls baselineStatement(t, ...) in place of the removed DDL constants, and it asserts that anchor_chain_hash is dropped, which matches the T-041 posture of keeping chain hashes out of durable state.

The query and runtime code in the changed files is unchanged. In the payout code, BeginGlobalPayout still uses FOR UPDATE on the withdrawal row, FOR SHARE on the recipient row, and a balance UPDATE that rejects negative results. Refunds are still written through the same transaction. No new attack surface appears in the visible code.

The security-relevant risk is that the migration baseline files were not supplied, so I could not check them. The following need review in that baseline:
- the unique partial index global_payout_external_id, which protects against duplicate external IDs (A-009 and the payment ledger);
- the quote-expiry and reconcile indexes;
- the expires_at NOT NULL column and its backfill, which BeginGlobalPayout relies on for quote expiry;
- the full contents of the deleted machine_inventory_schema.go, including any uniqueness and continuity constraints that bear on T-055 and T-058;
- the cache routing tables and their indexes.

If the baseline omits any of these, there are two possible regressions: duplicate payouts, or missing uniqueness on machine and credential associations. I could not verify either. Conclusions here are limited by that missing coverage.
